test_tlhash — TCP listen-socket hash routing (Phase 3 chunk 4)
==============================================================

Phase 3 chunk 4 closes Phase 3 of the netstack-quality plan: hash
the per-segment lookup `inet_tcp_find_listen(local_port)`. The
chained hash uses a separate table from chunk 3's connected-socket
hash, with `next_in_tcp_listen_bucket` linking listener slots
keyed on `local_port & 0xf`.

The risk this test catches: a buggy chain walk that returns the
first listener regardless of port match, a missed hash-remove on
listener close, or a hash function that maps two distinct listener
ports to the same slot.

Test plan:

  1. listen on 3 colliding ports (9000, 9016, 9032) — all hash
     to bucket 8 because the low 4 bits are 0. Each listener
     ends up linked off `inet_tcp_listen_hash[8]`.
  2. For each listener i:
       - tcp_connect 127.0.0.1:port[i] → loopback fast-path
         re-enters the segment dispatch; the SYN's destination
         port is matched against the listen hash.
       - tcp_accept on listener i → if hash returned the wrong
         listener, accept on listener i would block (no child)
         and the test would hang.
       - send tag byte ('A' + i), recv on child, echo +1, recv
         on connect-side, assert.
       - close child + connect-side fd. Listener stays open for
         the next iteration.
  3. close all 3 listeners. close_slot's listen-hash remove
     unlinks each. Re-listening (not done in this test, but
     covered indirectly when the test runs in a process recycle)
     would otherwise hit "phantom in-use" rejection.

5 TCP slots active at peak (3 listeners + 1 connect-side + 1
accepted child). Within the per-tid cap of 8.

    module oskit.bin.test_tlhash

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with}
    import oskit.net.{tcp_listen, tcp_accept, tcp_connect, tcp_send, tcp_recv, tcp_close}
    import std.net.{ipv4}


    print_dec(v: int)
        if v < 0
            puts("-")
            print_dec(-v)
            return
        if v >= 10
            print_dec(v / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("tlhash: failed ")
        print_dec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


    main(argv: []string)
        val LO = ipv4(127, 0, 0, 1)
        var ports: [3]int
        ports[0] = 9000
        ports[1] = 9016
        ports[2] = 9032

        var lfds: [3]int
        var i = 0
        while i < 3
            lfds[i] = tcp_listen(ports[i])
            if lfds[i] < 0
                fail(1 + i)
                return
            i += 1

        i = 0
        while i < 3
            val cfd = tcp_connect(LO, ports[i])
            if cfd < 0
                fail(10 + i)
                return

            var peer_ip: [4]byte
            var peer_port: [2]byte
            val sfd = tcp_accept(lfds[i], &peer_ip[0], &peer_port[0])
            if sfd < 0
                fail(20 + i)
                return

            var tx: [1]byte
            tx[0] = byte(int('A') + i)
            if tcp_send(cfd, &tx[0], 1) != 1
                fail(30 + i)
                return

            var rx: [1]byte
            if tcp_recv(sfd, &rx[0], 1) != 1
                fail(40 + i)
                return
            if int(rx[0]) & 0xff != int('A') + i
                fail(50 + i)
                return

            var tx2: [1]byte
            tx2[0] = byte(int(rx[0]) + 1)
            if tcp_send(sfd, &tx2[0], 1) != 1
                fail(60 + i)
                return

            var rx2: [1]byte
            if tcp_recv(cfd, &rx2[0], 1) != 1
                fail(70 + i)
                return
            if int(rx2[0]) & 0xff != int('A') + i + 1
                fail(80 + i)
                return

            tcp_close(sfd)
            tcp_close(cfd)
            i += 1

        i = 0
        while i < 3
            tcp_close(lfds[i])
            i += 1

        puts("tlhash: ok\n")
        exit()
